The Human-English reads the isomorphism as an interchange of limits in the category of continuous lattices and projections: \(D_{n+1} = [D_n \to D_n]\), \(j_{n+1} = [j_n \to j_n]\), and the function-space functor turns an inverse limit on the right and a direct limit on the left into an inverse limit, so the tail has the same limit. Nothing on this note carries out that interchange. The element-by-element isomorphism is [Theorem 4.4](../FunctionSpaceTower/1972_Theorem_4_4.md). Scott says the two proofs are the same and that he has not checked every detail of the functorial form. **Theorem:** no — the limit interchange is not formalized. **Proof:** no — Theorem 4.4 proves the isomorphism by mutual inversion of `embInfInf` and `projInfInf`.
